<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lille</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rijsel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lille is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> near the Belgian border, historically the chief town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walloon-flanders">Walloon Flanders</a>. Part of the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and later the Burgundian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, it was taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> in 1667 and became French by the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle in 1668, after which Vauban built its citadel. It became a great centre of the textile industry and is the capital of the Hauts-de-France region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lille.jpg" alt="Lille" width="320" height="429" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cereol89, Velvet · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lille_Collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lille lies along the Deûle river in the northern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-flanders">French Flanders</a> and close to the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. It is nicknamed in France the Capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, and it belongs to the historical region of Romance Flanders. The commune covers only 35 square kilometres, but together with its suburbs it forms a conurbation of several cities, including Roubaix, Tourcoing and Villeneuve-d'Ascq. It also forms part of a cross-border grouping with the Belgian cities of Mouscron, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kortrijk">Kortrijk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tournai">Tournai</a> and Menin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavations suggest the area was inhabited as early as 2000 BCE, in the districts now called Fives, Wazemmes and Vieux Lille. Its early inhabitants were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> such as the Menapians, the Morins, the Atrebates and the Nervians, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a> including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a>, the Frisians and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. A Battle of Lille was fought in 1054. From the 12th century the fame of the Lille cloth fair grew, and the city has been a merchant city since its origins and a manufacturing city since the 16th century.</p><p>As a garrison town, protected by its citadel, Lille was often besieged and belonged successively to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the Burgundian State, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and the Spanish Netherlands. In 1792, during the aftermath of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, Austrian forces besieged it for eight days; their artillery destroyed many houses and the main church, but the city, led by Mayor François André-Bonte, did not surrender. The Column of the Goddess, erected in 1842, commemorates the resistance.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> made Lille a great industrial capital, built mainly on textiles and mechanical industries. German forces occupied it on 13 October 1914 after a ten-day siege and heavy shelling that destroyed 882 apartment and office blocks and 1,500 houses. In July 1921 Albert Calmette and Camille Guérin announced at the Pasteur Institute in Lille the first anti-tuberculosis vaccine, known as BCG. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the city was occupied again and was liberated in early September 1944 by a British force largely of tanks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lille is the capital of the Hauts-de-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> region and the prefecture of the Nord department. Its old centre has 17th-century red brick town houses around the Grand'Place, and the belfry of its city hall is among the belfries inscribed as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a> in July 2005. Other sights include the citadel, the cathedral and the Palais des Beaux-Arts. Charles de Gaulle was born there. The annual Braderie, held on the first weekend of September, is thought to date from the twelfth century. Lille opened the first automatic light metro line in the world in 1983, and Eurostar trains have served it since 1994.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lille” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The county’s heartland was the Flemish-speaking north, but it also included French-speaking districts around <b>Lille</b>, Douai and Arras.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The melody now sung was composed the following year by Pierre De Geyter, a woodcarver and member of the French Workers' Party in the industrial city of <b>Lille</b>, for the party choir La Lyre des Travailleurs.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-internationale">The Internationale</a></li><li><blockquote>South of <b>Lille</b>, the army of Philip IV of France fought the Flemish militias that had defeated the French at Kortrijk two years earlier.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-18">18 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis XIV took Artois, <b>Lille</b>, Cambrai and other lands between 1659 and 1678.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-netherlands">Spanish Netherlands</a></li></ul></section>
